<h2>Topic 4: Volatility - ARCH and GARCH Models</h2><p><b>Estimated time:</b> ~35 minutes | <b>Objectives:</b> Explain volatility clustering, ARCH, GARCH, calculate conditional variance.</p>
<p>Returns are not equally jumpy every day. Big jumps follow big jumps. That is volatility clustering. ARMA cannot catch it - GARCH can.</p><div class='keybox'><p><b>ARCH(1):</b> sigma_t^2 = alpha_0 + alpha_1 e_{t-1}^2. Today's volatility depends on yesterday shock squared.</p><p><b>GARCH(1,1):</b> sigma_t^2 = alpha_0 + alpha_1 e_{t-1}^2 + beta sigma_{t-1}^2. Today's volatility depends on yesterday shock AND yesterday volatility. Most used in finance.</p><p><b>Example:</b> GARCH(1,1), alpha_0=0.0001, alpha_1=0.1, beta=0.8, e_{t-1}^2=0.0004, sigma_{t-1}^2=0.0005. sigma_t^2 = 0.0001 + 0.1x0.0004 + 0.8x0.0005 = 0.00054. Volatility = sqrt(0.00054) = 2.32%.</p></div>
